== Iskhal Megalopolis ==

{{main|Iskhal Megalopolis}}

The Iskhal Megalopolis is the most populous cultural region, with a population of over 24 million. It encompasses the southeastern coast along the [[Mesogean Sea]] and includes the [[South Hudur Metropolitan Administration]] and [[Greater Qallahere Metropolitan Administration]]. The region is characterised by a {{w|Mediterranean climate}} and is the principal economic and cultural centre of modern Burawa.

The region's major cities include [[Iskhal]], the largest city in Burawa and administrative centre for government-owned enterprises; [[Mazar]], the hub of private industry and technology; [[Docol]], site of the country's second-largest military base; and [[Qallahere]], headquarters of [[Burawi Oil]]. The port of [[Todgheer]] handles the majority of Burawa's maritime trade.

Historically, the region was home to the [[Republic of Mazar]]. The merger of Iskhal and Mazar in 403 AD formed the basis of the modern Burawi state. The region experienced significant bombing during the {{w|Great Adonian War}}, resulting in predominantly post-war development.

The economy is dominated by the private sector, with major industries including tourism, film production, technology, and financial services. The region is noted for its cosmopolitan character and comparatively lower levels of religious observance.

== Lake Haro ==

{{main|Lake Haro (region)}}

The Lake Haro region surrounds the [[Lake Haro System]] in southeastern Burawa, with a population of approximately 19 million. The region extends into [[Maianka]] and [[Sabia]]. The lake moderates the regional climate, producing mild conditions throughout the year.

Major cities include [[Badalga]], the largest city and a centre for heavy industry and shipbuilding; [[Jowhar]], historically significant as a capital of the Ufyen civilisation; and [[Khar]], a major industrial centre. The region also includes the historic city of [[Ufyen]].

The region was the heartland of the [[Kingdom of Ufyen]], which ruled from approximately 1000 BCE to 403 AD. Unlike the Iskhal Megalopolis, Lake Haro was not heavily bombed during the Great Adonian War and retains substantial historic architecture.

The regional economy is based on heavy industry, shipbuilding, manufacturing, and agricultural processing. The working-class population maintains strong connections to Ufyenite traditions. The Lake Haro is central to regional identity, supporting fishing and associated cultural practices.

== Buran River Valley ==

{{main|Buran River Valley}}

The Buran River Valley follows the [[Buran River]] from the [[Kushish Highlands]] to the [[Mesogean Sea]], with a population of over 16 million. The region includes the [[Greater Lanwaley Metropolitan Administration]] and [[Buran River Valley Metropolitan Administration]].

Geography ranges from highland valleys in the north to coastal lowlands in the south, with climate transitioning from {{w|subtropical highland}} to {{w|Mediterranean}}. [[Lanwaley]] is the largest city and the fastest-growing metropolitan area in Burawa. [[Laasqoray]] serves as the seat of the Buran River Valley Metropolitan Administration.

The Buran River served as the primary route for the southward expansion of Ufyenite civilisation. The regional economy is based on mining, heavy industry, and agricultural production. The population is notably multicultural, with significant immigrant communities.

== Dafar ==

{{main|Dafar}}

Dafar occupies the far southwestern corner of Burawa, bordering [[Kijura]] and the [[Aethiopean Ocean]], with a population of approximately 16 million. The region is centred on the [[Sago-Dare Metropolitan Administration]].

The region experiences a {{w|humid subtropical climate}} with higher rainfall than the eastern coast. Major cities include [[Sago]], the largest city and principal cultural centre; and [[Dare]] and [[Hooyal]], historically significant cities that gave their names to ancient kingdoms.

Historically, the region was home to the [[Kingdom of Dafar]] and [[Kingdom of Hooyal]], which merged to form the [[Kingdom of Sago]] circa 100 BCE. The area was incorporated into the [[Kingdom of Burawa]] during the medieval period. The regional economy is based on tourism, agriculture, and fishing.

== Western Coast ==

{{main|Western Coast}}

The Western Coast extends along the [[Aethiopean Ocean]] from the border with [[Kijura]] to the [[Gees Galbeedi]] peninsula, with a population of approximately 11 million. The region includes the [[Farada Metropolitan Administration]].

The region experiences a {{w|humid subtropical climate}} with oceanic influences. The coastline is characterised by bays, peninsulas, and sandy beaches. [[Gees Galbeedi]] (Western Cape) is a prominent geographic feature. The regional economy is based on fishing, maritime trade, and agriculture.

== Kankadadka-Samara Megalopolis ==

{{main|Kankadadka-Samara Megalopolis}}

The Kankadadka-Samara Megalopolis is the political capital region, centred on [[Kankadadka]] and [[Samara]], with a population of approximately 9 million. The region occupies the south-central coast at the transition between the [[Aethiopean Ocean]] and [[Mesogean Sea]].

[[Kankadadka]] served as the capital of the [[Kingdom of Burawa]] from the early medieval period and remains the official capital. [[Samara]] has grown to become the largest city in the metropolitan area. The region is the seat of the Burawi government and hosts major educational institutions including the [[People's University of Kankadadka]], [[University of Samara]], [[Samara Military University]], and [[Samara Technical University]].

== Dhulsarren ==

{{main|Dhulsarren}}

Dhulsarren occupies the west-central highland and valley areas of Burawa, centred on the [[Hooyal River]] valley, with a population of approximately 8 million. The region is characterised by mountainous terrain and a {{w|subtropical highland climate}}.

The region is known as "the Wheatlands" and is the principal grain-producing region of Burawa. It is a stronghold of the [[National Party of Burawa]]. Several cities bear Burawi Catholic names, including [[Idri]], [[Bishara]], and [[Pawl]].

== Central Coast ==

{{main|Central Coast}}

The Central Coast occupies the central portion of Burawa's southern coast along the [[Mesogean Sea]], with a population of approximately 5.4 million. The region features a {{w|Mediterranean climate}}.

The region is the centre of Burawa's petroleum industry, with offshore drilling operations. Major cities include [[Marka]], [[Jilib]], and [[Kaysoor]], the latter home to the [[National University of Kaysoor]] and historically a capital city.

== Dimar ==

{{main|Dimar}}

Dimar occupies the far northern portion of Burawa, extending beyond the [[Great Kaftian Mountain Range]] into the tropical savanna zone, with a population of approximately 5.4 million. The region experiences a {{w|tropical savanna climate}} with distinct wet and dry seasons.

The region was historically outside the main centres of Ufyenite civilisation, with a predominantly pastoralist population. The economy is based on livestock husbandry, mining, and agriculture. Several cities bear Burawi Catholic names, including [[Kedam]].

== Central Burawa ==

{{main|Central Burawa}}

Central Burawa, also known as Harada Sare (High Lakes), occupies the central highlands between the coastal lowlands and the mountain range, with a population of approximately 5.2 million. The region features a {{w|subtropical highland climate}} and contains seven major lakes.

The region is the religious heartland of Burawa, with the highest percentage of religious observance. Major lakes include [[Lake Harada]], [[Lake Waqooyi]], and [[Lake Koonfur]]. Several cities bear Burawi Catholic names, including [[Dawit]] and [[Qetura]].

== Eastern Burawa ==

{{main|Eastern Burawa}}

Eastern Burawa occupies the far eastern portion of Burawa along the [[Mesogean Sea]], extending to the border with [[Sabia]], with a population of approximately 4.6 million. The region includes the [[Adad Peninsula]].

The regional economy is based on natural gas extraction, manufacturing, and maritime trade with [[Sabia]]. Major cities include [[Bosaaso]] and [[Garoowe]].

== Waaq Region ==

{{main|Waaq Region}}

The Waaq Region occupies the northern mountainous interior of Burawa, centred on [[Buur Waaq]], the highest peak in [[Southwestern Kaftia]], with a population of approximately 1.4 million. It is the least populous cultural region.

The region features a {{w|subtropical highland climate}} at lower elevations and {{w|alpine climate}} near the summit of Buur Waaq (2,300 m). The region was historically the heartland of Ufyen religion, named after [[Waaq]], the sky deity. The mountainous terrain has limited development, and the region contains significant wilderness areas.

